{"command": "python3 - <<'PYEOF'\np='/testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nanchor='### Edge Cases'\nassert anchor in s\nstory5='''### User Story 5 - Time-Limited Entries (TTL) (Priority: P2)\n\nA developer creates a cache with an optional controllable timer (defaulting to\nthe standard monotonic clock) and stores entries with an optional time-to-live.\nEntries without a TTL never expire. Entries with a TTL expire at the absolute\nmoment `timer() + ttl`; a zero TTL is already expired at insertion time, and an\nentry is treated as expired at the exact expiry boundary. Expired entries are\npurged before `get`, `put`, `len`, `resize`, and invalidation, and purging\nnever changes the relative recency of surviving entries. Overwriting an entry\nreplaces its prior expiry. Invalidation counts only live (unexpired) entries.\nA `get` for a missing or expired key raises `KeyError`. Callers who never use\n`timer` or `ttl` observe exactly the previous behavior.\n\n**Why this priority**: Time-limited entries are a core requested capability of\nmilestone 3 and interact with every existing operation (purge-before-operation\nis a cross-cutting correctness rule); they depend on Story 1 storage and\nStory 2 recency/LRU semantics but are independently testable with a fake\ntimer.\n\n**Independent Test**: Build a cache with a fake timer; put an entry with a TTL\nand advance the fake clock to the exact expiry, then assert `get` raises\n`KeyError` and `len` is `0`; put with an invalid TTL (negative, `bool`,\nnon-numeric, non-finite) and assert `ValueError` with state unchanged; put\nwith `ttl=0` and assert the entry is immediately expired; verify a surviving\nentry\\'s recency is unchanged after a purge; verify `ttl=None` entries never\nexpire.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a cache built with a fake timer, **When** `put('a', 1, ttl=5)` is\n   called at `timer()==10`, **Then** the entry is live while `timer() < 15`\n   and `get('a')` raises `KeyError` at `timer() == 15` (exact boundary is\n   expired).\n2. **Given** a cache, **When** `put('a', 1, ttl=0)` is called, **Then** the\n   entry is immediately expired: `get('a')` raises `KeyError` and `len` does\n   not count it.\n3. **Given** an entry with a TTL, **When** the same key is re-put with\n   `ttl=None`, **Then** the entry no longer expires; re-putting with a new\n   finite TTL sets a new expiry from the new `timer()` value (prior expiry\n   replaced).\n4. **Given** one expired and one live entry, **When** `len`, `put`, `get`\n   (of the live key), `resize`, or `invalidate` is called, **Then** the\n   expired entry is removed first, the live entry survives, and the live\n   entry\\'s relative recency is unchanged by the purge.\n5. **Given** an entry carrying tag `'x'` that has expired, **When**\n   `invalidate('x')` is called, **Then** it returns `0` (expired entries are\n   not counted; only live entries are removed and counted).\n6. **Given** `put` with an invalid TTL (negative number, `bool`, non-numeric,\n   or non-finite such as infinity/NaN), **Then** `ValueError` is raised BEFORE\n   any state change (no insert, no replace, no recency change).\n7. **Given** a cache constructed with a custom `timer` callable, **Then** that\n   timer is the sole source of time for all expiry decisions; with the\n   default construction (`timer=None`) the standard monotonic clock is used.\n8. **Given** a cache and `put` calls that never pass `ttl` (and a constructor\n   that never passes `timer`), **Then** behavior is identical to the\n   pre-milestone-3 contract (entries never expire; all R01-R06 behavior\n   unchanged).\n\n'''\ns=s.replace(anchor, story5+anchor, 1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint('ok story5 inserted')\nPYEOF", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "ok story5 inserted\n"}